<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Millán de la Cogolla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Millán de la Cogolla is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with two monasteries, Suso and Yuso, that grew from the hermitage of the 6th-century saint Aemilian (Millán). The monastery was a major religious and landowning centre of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, and the Glosas Emilianenses written there contain some of the earliest written words in Castilian and Basque. Both monasteries are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-millan-de-la-cogolla.jpg" alt="San Millán de la Cogolla" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cenobio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valle-de-San-Mill%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
